    comparison: { caption: "Decisions to make before your first Israel trip", headers: ["Decision", "Useful starting point", "Check before paying"], rows: [["Where to stay", "Match bases to your main sights", "Hotel location, access and check-in"], ["Transport", "Plan complete daily routes", "Date, last service, luggage and parking"], ["Weekend arrival", "Arrange the airport-to-hotel journey", "Shabbat options and late reception"], ["Communication", "Data plus offline backup", "Device, activation and package coverage"], ["Days out", "One main focus per day", "Entry, opening window and return journey"]] },
    sections: [
      { id: "before-booking", title: "Check documents and conditions before booking", paragraphs: ["Use the official entry portal to check whether your passport needs an ETA-IL, a visa or another arrangement. Read the current requirements rather than assuming that a previous visit, an airline booking or someone else's nationality answers your case.", "Check current official travel advice and booking-change terms before committing to a route. Keep passport/permission records, accommodation details, insurance contacts and emergency information somewhere you can reach without a mobile connection."], sourceIds: ["guide-entry"] },
      { id: "bases", title: "Choose bases that match the trip you want", paragraphs: ["Jerusalem suits a stay focused on the Old City, religious sites and museums; Tel Aviv offers coastal neighbourhoods and a different city rhythm. Haifa or Tiberias can reduce repeated travel for northern plans, while an Ein Bokek stay can suit a dedicated Dead Sea break.", "Treat those as planning choices, not a requirement to sleep in every region. Place your intended visits on a map and compare an extra hotel change with a longer day trip. Allow time to pack, check out, travel and reach the next reception."], sourceIds: ["guide-original-travelTips"] },
      { id: "calendar", title: "Plan around Shabbat and holidays", paragraphs: ["Check Friday afternoon, Saturday and holiday journeys early. Regular transport and business hours change by place and operator; the Tel Aviv weekend network is a separate system, with a limited airport option explained in our Shabbat guide.", "Book an important connection around a service that is actually listed or confirmed. Check restaurant options and hotel entry for the same evening rather than solving transportation alone."], sourceIds: ["guide-weekend-711", "israel-rail"] },
      { id: "arrival-and-travel", title: "Work out your arrival and everyday transport", paragraphs: ["Allow for airport formalities before an onward departure. Use the right pickup instructions for your taxi or transfer and give an operator your full address, not only the destination city. For the return flight, follow current airline and airport arrival instructions.", "Public transport can help connect cities; walking, local transit and occasional taxis solve different last legs. A rental or private driver may fit rural or multi-stop days. Compare full costs, parking, hours and luggage requirements before choosing."], sourceIds: ["guide-iaa", "guide-gett"] },
      { id: "everyday-essentials", title: "Prepare data, payments and practical packing", paragraphs: ["Check phone compatibility and activation before buying a SIM/eSIM. Download maps and save booking details; do not rely on finding Wi-Fi every time you need an address or ticket.", "Carry a usable payment backup and check the final currency and total before approving a charge. Ask smaller vendors what they accept and retain receipts. Pack for the regions on your itinerary: a coastal day, hilltop evening and desert outing can need different layers and footwear."], sourceIds: ["guide-original-travelTips"] },
      { id: "respectful-visits", title: "Leave room for the place you are visiting", paragraphs: ["At religious sites, check dress and visitor instructions, keep photography considerate and respect prayer or service areas. Do not assume visitor access includes every chapel, sanctuary or ceremony.", "Avoid a checklist so full that there is no time for meals, queues or the walk back. Start with a few priorities each day and keep an alternative indoor or shorter activity for weather or operating changes."], sourceIds: ["guide-jerusalem-tourism"] },
    ],
